# Identifiers and local names

Single-letter names (`d`, `p`, `t`, `a`, `b`, `l`, `i`, `e`) make code harder to read and review. Prefer **short, explicit names** that state role or domain.

## Do

- Name values after what they hold: `trendDelta`, `trendPeriod`, `targetScore`, `layerA` / `layerB` in comparators, `layer` in filters.
- Use `index` (not `i`) in `forEach` / `map` when the index is used for logic or keys.
- Keep industry-standard abbreviations where everyone reads them the same way: `id`, `url`, `err`, `ctx` (only when conventional in that stack).

## Avoid

- One-letter locals except in extremely small scopes (e.g. a single-line arrow that stays obvious — still prefer a word when editing existing code).
- Cryptic shortenings: `n`, `x`, `o`, `h` for domain data.

## Comparators

Sort callbacks should use paired names: `layerA`/`layerB`, `left`/`right`, or `first`/`second` — not `a`/`b`.

When touching a file, align new or nearby code with this rule; do not rename unrelated symbols in the same PR unless asked.
